# Frontend Architecture & Performance Guide

When modifying code under `web/src/`, strictly follow the architecture rules and performance best practices below.

## 1. Architecture & Data Flow
- **Server-Authoritative**: game state is driven by the backend. The frontend updates its state by receiving `TickPayloadDTO`.
- **Store responsibilities**:
  - Follow the Pinia store responsibility split.
  - `world.ts` handles world-level orchestration (initialization, tick, time/celestial-phenomenon state) and must not keep growing into a God Store.
  - **New code should use the sub-stores (`map/avatar/event/ui/system`) directly**; the proxy fields on `world` are a compatibility layer.
- **Startup state machine**:
  - `App.vue` should stay an "assembly layer".
  - Root-level scene orchestration must be consolidated in `useAppShell`; avoid scattering multiple watchers/boolean branches across the root component.
  - The app shell must use a `scene + overlay` model: the underlying scene distinguishes at least `boot / splash / initializing / game`, and `SystemMenu` and `LoadingOverlay` are stacked on top as overlays.
  - The main game UI may only be driven by `scene === 'game'`; never infer "already in game" from inverted boolean combinations such as `!showSplash` or `!showLoading`.
  - Settings/load/about pages opened from `SplashLayer` still belong to the `splash` context; opening a menu must not implicitly expose the map, status bar, or other in-game UI.
  - The system menu uses a "shell + content + flow" split: `SystemMenuShell` owns the container, `SettingsPanel` owns the settings content, and `useSystemMenuFlow` owns the menu business flow; do not merge the three back into a monolithic `SystemMenu.vue`.
- **Socket layering**:
  - `api/socket.ts` is responsible for transport only (connect, reconnect, message subscription).
  - Message dispatch must go through `stores/socketMessageRouter.ts`; never branch on message type directly inside components.
- **Component / composable boundary**:
  - Large panels, status bar dialogs, and detail page components should stay "template presentation layers"; business state such as data loading, derived fields, navigation, submission, timers, and Pixi lifecycle should move down into `web/src/composables/*` or local `components/**/composables/*`.
  - Boundaries already extracted include: system startup overlay, map Pixi rendering, sect/region details, avatar portrait swapping, and the rankings/dynasty/mortals/time/sect relations/secret realm overviews; later changes should extend the existing composables rather than piling logic back into `.vue` files.
  - `web/src/components/game/composables/*` is for local composables in the game rendering domain (e.g. Pixi/map lifecycle); state logic reused across panels goes in `web/src/composables/*`.
- **Status bar dialogs and lazy loading**:
  - Non-first-screen status bar dialogs may be loaded on demand with `defineAsyncComponent` + `v-if`; high-frequency dialogs whose tests/interactions rely on synchronous mounting may keep a static import.
  - If a dialog component is first mounted by `v-if` only when `props.show === true`, any internal watcher that triggers API requests from `show` must use `{ immediate: true }`; otherwise the first open will not fetch data.
  - Such dialogs must have a regression test for "initial mount with `show=true` still requests data"; typical examples are the dynasty, rankings/World Martial Tournament, mortals overview, sect relations, time overview, and deceased avatars list dialogs.

## 2. Vue 3 Performance Best Practices
- **MUST**: for large read-only objects received from the backend (e.g. `AvatarDetail`, game state snapshots, map data), **you must use `shallowRef`** instead of `ref` or `reactive`. This avoids deep Proxy conversion and the resulting jank (10ms-100ms+ UI freezes).
- **Long list rendering**: for long lists (e.g. event logs, entity lists), prefer "pagination + incremental loading" or virtual scrolling. When the number of nodes rendered at once keeps growing, virtual scrolling is mandatory.
- **Memoization**: use `computed` for expensive derived state; avoid inline expressions or method calls in templates.
- **Performance baseline**: keep observable timing metrics for critical paths (initialization, event merging/loading); compare before and after refactors and make sure there is no regression.

## 3. Desktop & Browser
- **MUST**: the desktop and browser builds share the same frontend code. When watching canvas/container size changes, **never use `useWindowSize()`** (it treats the window size as the source of truth and easily drifts from the actual container size).
- **Always use `useElementSize(container)`** (based on `ResizeObserver`) so size changes always follow the actual container.

## 4. Error Recovery & Rendering
- **Anti-flicker design**: rely on the `boot` scene guard. Until settings hydration completes and the first backend `initStatus` response arrives, the frontend must stay on a pure black shell to prevent flicker.
- **Single exit for error handling**:
  - Use `utils/appError.ts` (`logError/logWarn`) for logging; avoid bare `console.error/warn` scattered through business code.
  - Show user-facing notices through `message` to avoid duplicate popups or inconsistent styles.
- **Pixi rendering optimization**:
  - **MUST**: **never put native PIXI objects (e.g. `Sprite`, `Container`) inside Vue deep reactive objects (`ref`, `reactive`)**. This breaks PIXI's internal strict equality (`===`) checks (e.g. `removeChild` stops working), causing memory leaks and logic bugs. To keep an instance reference, use a plain variable or `shallowRef`.
  - Dynamic effects (e.g. flowing water) must be driven independently by `PIXI.Ticker`, not by the Vue render loop.
  - The map is stored in a `shallowRef`. Tile rendering builds Pixi Sprites once in `onMounted`; static tiles do not take part in reactive updates.
  - Map region name layout (`MapLayer` / `utils/mapLabels.ts`) uses **collision avoidance** by default rather than "hide when overlapping". If a trade-off is required, keep labels visible first, then avoid collisions via a limited set of candidate positions.
  - Map region name layout must work for the locales enabled in `static/locales/registry.json` (currently `en-US`). Estimate bounding boxes from the actual text size and wrapping rules of the script in use; do not rely on a hardcoded single-script approximation.
  - The region name avoidance implementation must stay lightweight: prefer fixed candidate positions + a neighborhood index (e.g. spatial buckets) for local collision detection; never introduce per-frame solving, global physics simulation, or heavy algorithms that noticeably increase initialization/redraw cost.

## 5. Type & DTO Policy
- **When adding API fields**, update `types/api.ts` first, then normalize DTO -> Domain in `api/mappers/*`.
- **Do not spread `any`**: new code must not introduce unnecessary `any`; when a transition truly requires it, annotate its purpose and the point where it will be removed.

## 6. Static Asset Paths
- **Frontend-bundled static assets** (`web/public/*`) must never hardcode site-root absolute paths (e.g. `/icons/x.png`, `/sfx/x.ogg`, `/bgm/x.mp3`).
- Runtime-built paths to `public` assets must be generated by a shared helper based on `import.meta.env.BASE_URL`.
- Fixed images and icons inside components should go in `web/src/assets/*` and be imported as modules, letting Vite handle output paths and deployment prefixes.
- **Game runtime assets** (`/assets/*` served by the backend or proxy) are managed separately from frontend `public` assets; do not mix their directories or URL generation.

## 7. System Status Bar Colors
- Status bar / system-level navigation colors must separate "module base colors" from "state overlay colors"; base colors express which module an entry belongs to, while state colors are used only for dynamic changes such as active, available, or rarity.
- Avoid using yellow-orange heavily for module identity, reward feel, and active state at the same time; if one hue band carries multiple responsibilities, consolidate its meaning before adding new entries.
- Entries in the same lore cluster should share similar color families: information/knowledge leans cool blue, relations/organizations lean indigo, mortals/common folk lean teal-green, dynasty/rites lean ochre-gold, competition/honor leans gold.
